PAYTUNE MUSIC
Master Product Requirements Document (PRD)
Product: PAYTUNE Music
Product vision: A Pan-African music discovery, streaming, artist-following and creator-growth ecosystem.
Tagline: Pay Once, Tune Forever.
Document status: Proposed implementation specification
Priority: High
Primary platforms: Responsive web, Android and iOS-compatible experience
Primary audience: Music listeners, independent artists, record labels, music producers and administrators.

1. Product vision
PAYTUNE Music should become a powerful African music platform where listeners discover music from different countries, follow artists, listen to audio tracks, watch music videos, create playlists and support artists.

It should combine:
- Personalized music discovery.
- Music streaming.
- Artist profiles and channels.
- Audio and video content.
- Artist following.
- Likes, comments and playlists.
- Country-based music discovery.
- Artist analytics.
- Music publishing and management.
- Music purchases and other supported monetization.
- Platform-wide music administration.
The central experience:

Discover music → Play a track → Like it → Follow its artist → Visit the artist page → Watch videos → Listen to more tracks → Save music → Return for new releases.

PAYTUNE must maintain one connected artist identity throughout this experience.
2. Non-negotiable engineering requirements
These requirements apply to every Music module.
2.1 No mock data
- Do not hardcode example artists into production pages.
- Do not hardcode music tracks, album artwork or playlists.
- Do not generate fake follower counts or play counts.
- Do not simulate successful purchases.
- Do not pretend an artist was followed when the backend rejects the request.
- Do not use local arrays as substitutes for database records.
- Do not create sample records automatically in production.
- Do not show invented analytics when no data exists.
Development fixtures may exist only in isolated test environments and must never appear as production content.
2.2 Database is the source of truth
Every important operation must use the actual backend:
- Follow artist.
- Unfollow artist.
- Like track.
- Unlike track.
- Play track.
- Save track.
- Create playlist.
- Add track to playlist.
- Remove track from playlist.
- Publish track.
- Update artist profile.
- Retrieve artist videos.
- Retrieve artist audio.
- Retrieve listening history.
- Retrieve analytics.
A successful interface update must correspond to a successful backend operation.

Optimistic updates are allowed only with rollback when an operation fails.
2.3 Relationships must be real
Every relationship must use stable database identifiers and foreign keys.

Examples:
- One user can follow many artists.
- One artist can have many followers.
- One artist can publish many tracks.
- One artist can publish many videos.
- One album can contain many tracks.
- One track can belong to multiple genres.
- One user can like many tracks.
- One user can create multiple playlists.
- One playlist can contain multiple tracks.
- One track can have a corresponding music video.
- One artist profile must connect to the existing PAYTUNE user account.
Never identify an artist using a display name when an immutable artist ID is available.
2.4 Existing PAYTUNE systems must be reused
Use the existing:
- User authentication.
- Artist authentication and approval.
- User profiles.
- Artist profiles.
- Video system.
- Storage infrastructure.
- Payments and purchases.
- Notifications.
- Comments and moderation where compatible.
- Admin permissions.
- Audit logs.
Do not create another authentication system or another artist account system for Music.
2.5 Design defaults
- Default theme: White / light mode.
- Primary brand color: #FFB300.
- Text: dark charcoal.
- Background: white.
- Cards: white or light neutral.
- Borders: subtle neutral gray.
- Dark mode: optional user preference.
- Mobile-first responsive layouts.
- Consistent navigation and components across PAYTUNE.

